    "q-346953": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346953",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The cost for a company to produce $x$ bicycles, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nC(x)={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+300.\n$$\n\nGiven that the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, how many bicycles should the company produce per week to minimize its weekly cost?\n\nThe company should produce\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles per week to minimize its weekly cost.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $x$ represent the number of bicycles produced per week. Since the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 10.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $C(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+48,\n$$\n\nand solving ${C}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-10x+16) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)(x-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=8$ gives a minimum value of $C(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6x-30\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=8$ into ${C}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{″}(2) & =12-30=-18<0, \\\\ {C}^{″}(8) & =48-30=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive at $x=8,$ the point $x=8$ is a local minimum of $C(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=8$ and $x=10.$ Computing $C(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(0) & =300 \\\\ C(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+300=236\\,✓ \\\\ C(10) & =(10)^{3}-15(10)^{2}+48(10)+300=280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is the global minimum of $C(x),$ and the company should produce $8$ bicycles per week to minimize weekly cost.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
