    "q-119028": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119028",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "A school is organizing a trip to a theme park. The trip is offered to at least $12$ kids, and it can be attended by at most $20$ kids. The cost of each ticket is $$80$ if there are exactly $12$ kids. If there are more than $12$ kids, the price per ticket drops by $$4$ for each kid in excess of $12.$ Every kid pays the same amount for going on the trip.\n\nHow many kids must go on the trip for the theme park to maximize its revenue?",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that if there are $12+x$ kids, then the price per ticket is $80-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of kids}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(80-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per ticket}} \\\\  & =(12+x)(80-4x) \\\\  & =960-48x+80x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =960+32x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there can only be between $12$ and $20$ kids, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\le x+12\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=8$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=32-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=4$ lies in the interval $0<x<8.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=4$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=4$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the theme park should admit $12+4=16$ kids to maximize its revenue.",
