    "q-87569": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87569",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "An auto parts store sells $x$ units of a certain product at a price per unit $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $1$ and $6$ units of the product each day, how many units should the store sell to maximize its revenue?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+63x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+63,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+63 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+21 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=3$ and $x=7.$ However, the value $x=7$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only new candidate is $x=3.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,3$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+63(1)+11=60 \\\\ R(3) & =(3)^{3}-15(3)^{2}+63(3)+11=92\\,✓ \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-15(6)^{2}+63(6)+11=65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the auto parts store should sell $3$ units.",
