    "q-119021": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119021",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A clothes store sells $x$ hundreds of pants at a price $p(x),$  in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)=200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $500$ and $1\\,000$ pants per month, how many pants should it sell monthly to maximize its revenue?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ pants, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =200x+40{x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $5\\le x\\le 10,$  since $x=5$  corresponds to $5\\cdot 100=500$  and $x=10$ corresponds to $10\\cdot 100=1\\,000.$  Therefore, $x=5$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $5<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=200+80x-10{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-80x-200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2$ and $x=10.$ However, the value $x=-2$  is not in the permitted domain. Moreover, $x=10$ is already a candidate, so our candidates are  $x=5$ and $x=10.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(5) & =200(5)+40(5)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{5}^{3}≈1\\,583 \\\\ R(10) & =200(10)+40(10)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{10}^{3}≈2\\,667\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the clothes store should sell $1\\,000$ pants monthly.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
