    "q-347034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347034",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A private art studio can enroll between $8$ and $14$ students in a painting workshop. The fee that each student pays is $$72$ if exactly $8$ students enroll, and if more than $8$ students are enrolled, the fee drops by $$4$ for each additional student. Each student pays the same fee.\n\nHow many students must the workshop have to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize its revenue, the workshop must have\n{{field-1}}\nstudents.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that if there are $8+x$ students, then the fee is $72-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(8+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of students}}\\times {{(72-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fee}} \\\\  & =(8+x)(72-4x) \\\\  & =576-32x+72x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =576+40x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the workshop can only have between $8$ and $14$ students, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n8\\le x+8\\le 14⇒0\\le x\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=40-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40-8x & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the domain $0<x<6.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ gives a maximum. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the workshop must have $8+5=13$ students enrolled to maximize its revenue.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
