    "q-346992": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346992",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "A photography studio books $x$ photo sessions per day at a price per session $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the studio books between $1$ and $6$ sessions per day, how many sessions should it book to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize revenue, the studio should book\n{{field-1}}\nphoto sessions per day.",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x+45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-18x+15,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ However, $x=1$ is already a candidate, so the new candidate is $x=5.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,\\,5,$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-9(1)^{2}+15(1)+45=1-9+15+45=52\\,✓ \\\\ R(5) & =(5)^{3}-9(5)^{2}+15(5)+45=125-225+75+45=20 \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-9(6)^{2}+15(6)+45=216-324+90+45=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize revenue, the studio should book $1$ photo session per day.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
