    "q-347242": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347242",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A plant spends $C(x)=150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousands of dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product per month. The plant sells the product for $$200\\,000$ per unit. Given that the plant can produce between $2$ and $9$ units per month, how many units should the plant produce per month to maximize its profit?\n\nThe optimal number of plant production is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product, the plant has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ thousands of dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+180x-150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $2\\le x\\le 9.$ Therefore, $x=2$ and $x=9$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $2<x<9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-48x+180,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-48x+180 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-16x+60 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =6,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the critical points $x=6$ and $x=10,$ only $x=6$ is within the permitted domain.\n\nAll that's left now is to check if $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =6x-48 \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-12<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at $x=6$ is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a maximum.\n\nOur candidates are $x=2,x=6$ and $x=9.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =(2)^{3}-24(2)^{2}+180(2)-150=122 \\\\ P(6) & =(6)^{3}-24(6)^{2}+180(6)-150=282\\,✓ \\\\ P(9) & =(9)^{3}-24(9)^{2}+180(9)-150=255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the plant will have a maximum profit level when it produces $6$ units per month.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
