    "q-87573": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87573",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200.$ The cost of providing the course is $C(x)=200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4},$ where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates' test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ participants, the computer science center has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-0.3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $3\\le x\\le 13.$ Therefore, $x=3$ and $x=13$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $3\\ge x\\ge 13.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 1.2{x}^{2}(10-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ However, the values $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=10.$\n\nLet's check if $x=10$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =24x-3.6{x}^{2} \\\\ {P}^{″}(10) & =240-360=-120<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at  $x=10$ is negative, the point $x=10$ is a maximum. \n\nOur candidates are $x=3,x=10$ and $x=13.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =4(3)^{3}-0.3(3)^{4}=83.7 \\\\ P(10) & =4(10)^{3}-0.3(10)^{4}=1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ P(13) & =4(13)^{3}-0.3(13)^{4}=219.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum profit is $$1\\,000.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
