    "q-65867": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65867",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A factory spends $C(x)={x}^{4}+13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121\\,000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=121\\cdot x=121x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =121x-({x}^{4}+13x) \\\\  & =108x-{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=4$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<4.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=108-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 108-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=3$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(3)=-12(3)^{2}=-108<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that $x=3$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=3$ and $x=4.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =108(0)-{0}^{4}=0 \\\\ P(3) & =108(3)-{3}^{4}=243\\,✓ \\\\ P(4) & =108(4)-{4}^{4}=176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $$243\\,000.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
