    "q-119098": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119098",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(5-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(3+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$",
