    "q-316188": {
      "dom_id": "step-q316188",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ ride passes per day, the park has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=15x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the park is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{700}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =15x-(700+2x) \\\\  & =13x-700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=500$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=13,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=500.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =13(0)-700=-700, \\\\ P(500) & =13(500)-700=6500-700=5800\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of ride passes that maximizes the park’s profit is $500.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
